{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["And his saying <b>with integrity of my heart</b> etc. — he intended to respond to the suspicion that she is a married woman and her husband is another man, and what did he gain by Abraham's saying of her, ״she is my sister״? Therefore he said <b>with integrity of my heart</b> etc. — I thought she was unattached and I am not liable to the death penalty. And his saying <b>and with innocence of my hands</b> — he intended to remove liability to the death penalty from the law of robbery, as we say in Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 57a), and Rambam ruled it in chapter nine, and this is his language: a son of Noah is liable for robbery etc., and whether one robs etc. or kidnaps a person, end quote. And it is not specifically kidnapping but the same applies to robbery, for in the case of a beautiful woman the law of kidnapping a person is stated in the baraita (there), and a beautiful woman is robbed, not kidnapped, and for the purpose of inclusion Rambam wrote גונֵב, and so the Kessef Mishneh wrote. And if so, from the moment Avimelekh took her he became liable to death; therefore he said <b>and with innocence of my hands</b>, for with their consent I thought the matter would proceed, and above all it would be accounted to her as her brother that the king married her."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:29.669280442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}